Z||000009||AL||12-16-1999||02:04 AM||||you know it's funny, i remember a while back,when the whiteboys used to have ther cars raised in the back and low in the front,they used to think they were cool, and when they saw a mexican comming with his dropped ride they would laugh and call us names.well look who's laughing now! it seems that everybody wants to be like us.low to the ground!!!||||reg||1
Z||000010||Patrick||12-16-1999||02:45 AM||||<BR>Well, not quite everybody AL. I still like my cars raised in the back.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Being on the west coast, British Columbia is heavily influenced by trends from the three coastal states south of here, especially California. The only common term I've ever used myself for a lowered car is a <B>low-rider</B>. It's hardly imaginative, but it is descriptive. I don't mind the cars being lowered as much as I hate the look of small wheels and skinny tires all around. Give me big honkin' wide tires in the back anytime! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||14
Z||000011||Monkeyman||12-16-1999||03:08 AM||||Low pro tires are cool as long as they're wide AND (this important!) stay inside the wheel wells.  Everyone's seen the (I can't think of a word that won't offend somebody) cars that have the 45" wide wheel/tires that stick out about 3 feet.  BUTT-UGLY!!!!!!||||reg||1
Z||000012||Wolfhound||12-16-1999||06:19 AM||||Monkeyman, That's what we call the Hot Wheels Generation. What about the tiny wheels outside the wheel wells. Cut your gas mileage in half and double your speedo reading in one shot.||||reg||12
